README-NAMES -- twenty names here are names you already haveYour OS-9 came with its own utilities, and twenty programs on this diskcarry one of their names.  Nothing here overwrites anything: this is a diskyou mount, not an installer.  But a name can only mean one thing at a time,and OS-9 decides which in two places -- so this file says which names, andwhat decides.WHAT DECIDES WHICH ONE RUNS---------------------------  1. Your execution search.  A bare `dir' is looked for in the execution     directory and then along your PATH, in order.  Put this disk's CMDS     ahead of yours and this disk's `dir' answers; put it after and yours     does.  A full pathlist -- `/h0/CMDS/dir' -- never guesses.  2. Residency, which is the one that surprises people.  Once a module is     in memory OS-9 finds it BY MODULE NAME, whatever path you type.  So     `load' one of these and it answers for that name everywhere, until     `unlink' lets it go.  It is the same mechanism that makes `load' useful     for the programs that fork a helper by bare name (DOC/README-RUNNING),     and it cuts both ways.     `mdir' lists what is resident, and that is the place to look when a     familiar command starts behaving unfamiliarly.THE TWENTY----------Each line is what THIS disk's program is.  What yours is, you know.  chown      set the user half of a file's owner, by number  compress   compress and uncompress, Lempel-Ziv-Welch  dir        a directory listing with its own options (`-e' adds more)  dump       a hex dump of a file, or of a module in memory (-m)  expand     GNU expand: tabs to spaces  grep       GNU grep 2.0  help       a help system reading its articles from SYS/HELP  kermit     OS-9 Kermit 1.5, serial file transfer  make       a make that maintains a target  mshell     a menu shell  mv         GNU mv (fileutils 3.13)  printenv   print the environment  setime     set the system time, prompting YYMMDDHHMMSS  su         GNU su  tar        GNU tar 1.10  umacs      uMacs 1.0 -- MicroEMACS in 45K  undel      bring back a deleted file  what       inventory the expansion cards in a GEPARDEight more sit in subdirectories, where nothing will reach them unless youput those directories on your PATH: `cc1plus', `cc2', `cc2plus', `cccp2',`collect', `gcc2' and `gpp' under CMDS/GCC2 and CMDS/GCC139, and `inetd'under CMDS/WN.  DOC/README-GCC is about those.NOT SHADOWS-----------`cio', `csl', `math' and `math881' in CMDS are Microware's own modules,here by permission -- the same thing under the same name, not anotherprogram wearing it.  SOURCES.txt records the permission.HOW THIS LIST WAS MADE----------------------By comparing every filename under this disk's CMDS with the utility set ofan OS-9/68K system disk, and keeping the names in both.  A system older ornewer than that one will differ a little at the edges; the two rules at thetop are what matter, and they do not change.